{"name":"Ryu’s Kimono","hex":"#f3f1e1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ryu","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Okinawan."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Japanese school of a specific discipline, usually martial arts."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ryu"},{"term":"kimono","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A traditional Japanese T-shaped, wrapped-front garment with square sleeves and a rectangular body, now generally worn only on formal occasions.","A long robe-like garment in Western fashion, which may be open at the front, loosely inspired by the Japanese garment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kimono"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kimono","blocks":[{"text":"The kimono  is a traditional Japanese garment and the national dress of Japan. The kimono is a wrapped-front garment with square sleeves and a rectangular body, and is worn left side wrapped over right, unless the wearer is deceased. The kimono is traditionally worn with a broad sash, called an obi, and is commonly worn with accessories such as zōri sandals and tabi socks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Two_young_maiko_in_Seirai-in_Temple_02.jpg/330px-Two_young_maiko_in_Seirai-in_Temple_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kimono"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Young_Woman_at_Kennin-ji_Zen_Temple_-_Kyoto_-_Japan_-_01_%2847929418887%29.jpg/500px-Young_Woman_at_Kennin-ji_Zen_Temple_-_Kyoto_-_Japan_-_01_%2847929418887%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Young_Woman_at_Kennin-ji_Zen_Temple_-_Kyoto_-_Japan_-_01_%2847929418887%29.jpg/1280px-Young_Woman_at_Kennin-ji_Zen_Temple_-_Kyoto_-_Japan_-_01_%2847929418887%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&